<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">East Tyrol</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> is a district of the Austrian state of Tyrol, centred on Lienz. It belonged to the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a> until 1500, when it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and was joined to Tyrol, and it has been cut off from North Tyrol since 1919, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Its high valleys include part of the Hohe Tauern national park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/east-tyrol.jpg" alt="East Tyrol" width="320" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original file: PSIplusDerivative work: Alphathon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tirol-S%C3%BCdtirol-Trentino_with_extraprovincial_comuni.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> is an exclave of the state of Tyrol and is coterminous with the administrative district of Lienz. Within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> it borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> to the east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> to the north, and it shares borders with the Italian provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a> to the west and Belluno to the south. It is separated from the Tyrolean district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schwaz">Schwaz</a> in North Tyrol by a 9.5 km stretch where South Tyrol meets the Pinzgau region of Salzburg. The High Tauern National Park lies partly in East Tyrol, as do the Großglockner, at 3,798 m the highest mountain in Austria, and the Großvenediger.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area around the former Roman municipium of Aguntum was held from the 12th century by the Counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a>, who lived at Lienz and inherited the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> in 1253. Tyrol itself passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a> in 1363, but the Gorizian counts kept Lienz until their line died out, after which Emperor Maximilian I joined it to Austrian Tyrol.</p><p>Its present position goes back to the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>. Under the Treaty of London of 1915, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was promised the Tyrolean lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brenner-pass">Brenner Pass</a>, and in November 1918 the Italian army occupied all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> with 20,000 to 22,000 soldiers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye-1919">Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye</a> of 1919 obliged German Austria to cede the southern part of the Princely County of Tyrol, the present provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a> and parts of Belluno. After the German annexation of Austria in 1938, East Tyrol was attached to the Reichsgau Kärnten; after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it lay in the British zone of occupation and was returned to Tyrol in 1947.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “East Tyrol” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the First World War, the south, including Bolzano and Trento, went to Italy in 1919, while North and <b>East Tyrol</b> remain an Austrian state.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-tyrol">County of Tyrol</a></li></ul></section>
